  "text": "Radiation Oncology | Gulf Coast Veterinary Specialists (GCVS)\nSkip to Primary Content\nFind A Location\nContact Us\nContact Us\n24/7 Emergency Room\n24/7 Emergency Room\nReferral Portal\nCareers\nPay Online\nAbout Us\nAbout Us\nGCVS Houston Team\nGCVS NASA Parkway Team\nCareers\nGCVS Gives Back\nNews & Blog\nServices\nEmergency\nCritical Care\nAnesthesia & Pain Management\nAvian & Exotics\nDermatology & Allergy\nDiagnostic Imaging\nInternal Medicine\nI-131 Treatment\nNeurology\nMedical Oncology\nRadiation Oncology\nOphthalmology\nSports Medicine & Rehabilitation\nSurgery & Orthopedics\nPet Owners\nFAQ\nPatient Forms\nPay Online\nFinancing\nPet Loss Support\nPharmacy & Refills\nReferring Veterinarians\nReferral Portal\nReferral Support\nReferral Form\nCase Studies\nSymposium\nTeleradiology\nFind A Location\n24/7 Emergency Room\nContact Us\nRadiation Oncology\nA common component of cancer care is radiation therapy. Depending on the tumor type and location, radiation therapy may be the sole treatment or part of a multi-modal treatment protocol for your pet’s cancer. There are several types of radiation therapy. One of the most frequently used forms is external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), which uses high-energy beams directed at the tumor site to kill cancer cells.\nThis service is offered at our\nHouston\nlocation.\nCommon tumors treated with EBRT include but are not limited to, tumors derived from the skin, nose, brain, mouth, bones, and gastrointestinal system.\nOur radiation oncology team uses advanced cross-sectional imaging, such as CT and MRI, to localize the tumor. A linear accelerator produces and focuses a beam of radiation precisely where the tumor is located.\nCancers We Treat:\n(But Not Limited To)\nNasal lymphoma\nMast cell tumor\nTransitional cell carcinoma\nNon-splenic hemangiosarcoma\nAnal gland carcinoma\nOsteosarcoma\nSoft-tissue sarcoma\nOral tumors\nMalignant melanoma\nNasal tumors\nBrain and spinal cord tumors\nOur Radiation Capabilities:\nVarian Halcyon Linear Accelerator with stereotactic radiation therapy (SRT) capability. SRT involves the delivery of a high-intensity radiation beam to treat certain macroscopic tumors.\nWe are unable to utilize stereotactic radiation therapy in all tumors; therefore, we offer full-course radiation therapy and palliative radiation therapy protocols.\nHave Questions?\nVisit Our FAQ Page!\nLearn More\nRadiation Oncology\nDustin Lewis\nDVM, MBA, DACVR (RO)\nView Profile\nJane Rowlands\nDVM\nView Profile\nDustin Lewis\nDVM, MBA, DACVR (RO)\nView Profile\nJane Rowlands\nDVM\nView Profile\nAbout Us\nGCVS Houston Team\nGCVS NASA Parkway Team\nPatient Forms\nReferral Portal\nOur Locations\nAccessibility\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCareers\nEmergency\nCookie Preferences\nDo Not Sell or Share My Information\n©\nCopyright\n2026",
